(window.COURSE_SOURCES = window.COURSE_SOURCES || {})["camping-basics"] = "\ufeffRead in full: the NatureScot camping guide, the Dartmoor camping page, the England and Wales Countryside Code, the NI Countryside Code, Leave No Trace Ireland's camping page, the Leave No Trace principles, REI's camping-for-beginners, tent choice, tent set-up, condensation, sleeping-bag ratings and camp-sleep pages, the CDC water page, the BMC stove page, the nidirect carbon monoxide page, the FSA camping food post, the NHS hypothermia page, the Mountain Rescue England and Wales page, the Met Office mountain safety and wind chill pages, the OS map-reading booklet (text extraction was imperfect), the OS compass guide and the OS Naismith post.\n\nRead in part or not substantively: REI's sleeping pads page (read in part), the Mountaineering Ireland homepage (homepage only), the Leave No Trace Ireland homepage (cookie notice only; the camping page was read). Not found or not read: an NHS tick bites page (404) and an official Irish mountain rescue page. Ticks and basic first aid are now covered by the sources below. The course says so where it matters.\n\n## Book sources (19/11/2026)\n\nFirst review: Scottish Outdoor Access Code 2005, Countryside Code (GOV.UK), OS Map reading made easy and the CDC Yellow Book water chapter, each read in full.\n\nSecond review, 19/11/2026:\n\n- Darwall v Dartmoor National Park Authority [2025] UKSC 20: read in full. Module 1 now teaches the legal basis of Dartmoor wild camping (section 10(1), Dartmoor Commons Act 1985, subject to the byelaws).\n- UKHSA Be Tick Aware toolkit (2023): read in full. Module 6 now teaches ticks and Lyme disease; Module 2 adds ticks to pitch choice. The toolkit gives two different tick species counts, so the course states none.\n- Aerie Wilderness Medicine, 15th edition (US): read in part, 19 of 68 chapters. Clinical first-responder skills, US law, altitude illness, medications and survival chapters were skipped with reasons. It feeds hypothermia staging and the wrap (Module 3), trench foot (Module 3), stove burns and carbon monoxide (Module 4), thunderstorms (Module 5), and a new Module 6 lesson on blisters, burns, sprains, stings and heat. UK emergency numbers come from Mountain Rescue England and Wales, not the US manual. Aerie's boil times are internally inconsistent, so Module 4 keeps the CDC figures.\n\nFindings across all reviews: 68 logged; 56 applied, 7 confirmed, 3 avoided, 2 skipped.\nStill web-only: tents, sleeping bags and pads.\n";
(window.COURSE_SOURCEMAP = window.COURSE_SOURCEMAP || {})["camping-basics"] = {"modules": {"1": "Where you may camp, and leaving no trace", "2": "Shelter: choosing, pitching, staying dry", "3": "The sleep system and staying warm", "4": "The camp kitchen: stoves, water, food", "5": "Navigation, timing and weather limits", "6": "Safety, illness and calling for help", "cap": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp"}, "title": "", "intro": "﻿Read in full: the NatureScot camping guide, the Dartmoor camping page, the England and Wales Countryside Code, the NI Countryside Code, Leave No Trace Ireland's camping page, the Leave No Trace principles, REI's camping-for-beginners, tent choice, tent set-up, condensation, sleeping-bag ratings and camp-sleep pages, the CDC water page, the BMC stove page, the nidirect carbon monoxide page, the FSA camping food post, the NHS hypothermia page, the Mountain Rescue England and Wales page, the Met Office mountain safety and wind chill pages, the OS map-reading booklet (text extraction was imperfect), the OS compass guide and the OS Naismith post. Read in part or not substantively: REI's sleeping pads page (read in part), the Mountaineering Ireland homepage (homepage only), the Leave No Trace Ireland homepage (cookie notice only; the camping page was read). Not found or not read: an NHS tick bites page (404) and an official Irish mountain rescue page. Ticks and basic first aid are now covered by the sources below. The course says so where it matters.", "standard": false, "books": [{"key": "aerie-wm", "name": "Wilderness Medicine, 15th edition (Aerie Backcountry Medicine)", "by": "", "words": 47770, "status": "full", "read": 19, "skipped": [{"reason": "Front matter, contents, layout notes, anatomy figure pages (no text) and glossary; no chapter teaching text.", "titles": ["Front matter", "Wilderness Medicine - 14th Edition", "Table of Contents", "Section One:\nIntroduction", "Manual Layout", "Abdominal and Thoracic Anatomy", "Skeletal Structure Anatomy", "Cardiopulmonary Blood Flow", "Glossary"]}, {"reason": "US legal aspects of wilderness medicine (Good Samaritan, consent under US law); does not apply to the UK and Ireland, where the course defers to official UK and Irish sources. Holds chapter text.", "titles": ["Pertinent Legal Aspects of Wilderness Medicine"]}, {"reason": "Clinical first-responder skills (patient assessment, airway, CPR, bleeding control, trauma, spinal care, fractures, splinting, litters). A beginner camping course does not teach these; it points learners to a hands-on first-aid course instead. Holds chapter text.", "titles": ["Patient Assessment", "Primary Survey", "Primary Survey (ABCDEs)", "Secondary Survey", "Vital Signs", "Patient History (SAMPLE/OPQRST)", "Written and Verbal Patient Reports (SOAP note/ACT-MIST report)", "SOAP Note - Front", "Airway Management", "Airway Maneuvers", "Backcountry CPR", "Stress Injuries and Psychological First Aid", "Improving Rescuer Performance Under Stress", "Bleeding and Shock", "External Bleeding", "Internal Bleeding", "Chest Trauma", "Head Injuries", "Spinal Injuries", "Head, Eyes, Ears, Nose and Throat (HEENT)", "Musculoskeletal Trauma", "Splinting", "Fractures", "Fractures with Special Considerations", "Litters for Patient Evacuation"]}, {"reason": "Medical emergencies, high-altitude illness (UK and Irish hills are below altitude-illness heights), drowning, triage, medications, survival kits and helicopter landing zones are outside the course scope. Holds chapter text.", "titles": ["Medical Emergencies", "Cardiac Emergencies", "Abdominal Medical Emergencies", "Pharmacology of Epinephrine", "Asthma", "Diabetic Emergencies", "High Altitude Illnesses", "Drowning", "Triage and Multiple Casualty Incidents", "Stress Management", "Medications", "Preparedness and Survival Considerations", "Survival Kit Contents", "Helicopter Landing Zone"]}], "sections": 68, "pct": 36, "unread": [], "findings": 18, "verdicts": {"contradicted": 2, "extended": 13, "confirmed": 1, "note": 2}, "review": "complete", "modules": [], "note": "", "prov": {"added": "2026-11-19", "source_file": "Aerie Wilderness Medicine 15th edition.pdf", "found_by": "source-discovery", "url": "https://www.aeriemedicine.com/wp-content/uploads/2025/06/Aerie-15th-Edition-Manual-15-02.pdf", "query": "Source Discovery, 19/11/2026"}, "states": {"applied": 15, "confirmed": 1, "avoided": 2}, "uses": [{"module": 3, "title": "The sleep system and staying warm", "lessons": [{"title": "A warm night, and spotting hypothermia", "chapters": ["Hypothermia (ch-047.txt)", "Cold Injuries (ch-048.txt)"], "points": ["In the field you judge how serious it is by alertness, movement and shivering, because a thermometer is rarely to hand. Someone who is shivering but alert and moving normally is cold-stressed, not yet hypothermic. Mild hypothermia adds hard shivering, clumsiness, poor decisions and not caring. When shivering weakens or stops and the person turns drowsy, it is moderate or worse.", "Most people who feel cold are not hypothermic, but the danger is that early signs get ignored while the group pushes on. Being dehydrated, exhausted, underfed or drinking alcohol all make it more likely.", "For a cold-stressed camper: get out of the wind, put on dry layers, give high-calorie food and a warm drink, then let them move about. For mild hypothermia, handle them gently and have them sit or lie down for at least 30 minutes. Give food or drink only while they are fully alert, and get help if there is no improvement after 30 minutes.", "Build a hypothermia wrap from camp kit: a sleeping pad underneath, a plastic or foil layer, a sleeping bag covering the head, and a windproof outer layer. Wrapped warm bottles on the upper chest, sides and back add heat, but never against bare skin, because cold skin burns easily. If shelter is more than 30 minutes away, take off wet clothing before wrapping; if it is closer, wrap straight away.", "Days of cold, wet feet can cause trench foot, a non-freezing injury, though symptoms rarely appear in under 4 days. Dry your feet every night and keep one pair of dry socks only for sleeping."]}]}, {"module": 4, "title": "The camp kitchen: stoves, water, food", "lessons": [{"title": "Stoves and fire: the safe way to cook", "chapters": ["Burns (ch-031.txt)"], "points": ["Camp stove accidents are one of the most common causes of burns outdoors, so set the stove on ground as flat and secure as you can find and wear closed-toe shoes around it. Module 6 covers burn first aid."]}, {"title": "Carbon monoxide: the silent hazard", "chapters": ["Carbon Monoxide (CO) Poisoning (ch-053.txt)"], "points": ["It is so dangerous because blood takes up carbon monoxide over 200 times more readily than oxygen, and a healthy person can take more than four hours to clear it. Rescuers can be overcome within minutes, so protect yourself first: open the tent from outside and get everyone into fresh air. Suspect it when several people in a confined space become confused or unwell together."]}, {"title": "Making water safe", "chapters": ["Water Purification and Filtration (ch-059.txt)"], "points": ["Two limits to remember: boiling kills germs but does not remove farm chemicals or pesticides washed into a stream, and chemical tablets work more slowly in cold water and in water carrying debris."]}]}, {"module": 5, "title": "Navigation, timing and weather limits", "lessons": [{"title": "How long will it take? Naismith's rule and the weather limits", "chapters": ["Lightning (ch-050.txt)"], "points": ["Count the seconds from a flash to its thunder: sound travels about 1.6 km every five seconds. If the gap is 30 seconds or less, move to safer ground and stay away from exposed places until 30 minutes after the last thunder. A building or a hard-top car is the safest place.", "Get off summits and ridges, and keep away from lone trees, rock outcrops and water, staying at least twice a tree's height from it. Spread a group 6 to 12 m apart, then crouch on a sleeping pad with feet together. Moving to lower ground does more than changing posture.", "Sound travels about 1.6 km every five seconds, so 12 seconds is roughly 4 km away (our arithmetic). That is inside the 30-second warning, so get off exposed ground and away from lone trees, and spread the group out."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Safety, illness and calling for help", "lessons": [{"title": "Calling for help", "chapters": ["Backcountry Decision Making (ch-055.txt)", "Evacuation Criteria (ch-056.txt)"], "points": ["Before you call, stop and think. Check that the scene is safe so you do not become another casualty, count everyone in the group, then look at the casualty, the weather, the light and what kit you have before deciding whether to stay or go.", "Get help out for anyone who cannot bear weight, has a change in alertness or breathing, or still has fever or vomiting after 24 hours."]}, {"title": "Minor injuries and heat: blisters, burns, sprains and stings", "chapters": ["Specific Wounds (ch-029.txt)", "Burns (ch-031.txt)", "Sprains and Strains (ch-033.txt)", "Evacuation Criteria (ch-056.txt)", "Allergic Reactions and Anaphylaxis (ch-043.txt)", "Heat Emergencies (ch-052.txt)", "First Aid Kits (ch-060.txt)"], "points": ["A blister starts as a hot spot, a warm, sore patch. Prevent it with well-fitting, broken-in boots laced properly, wool or synthetic socks rather than cotton, dry feet and trimmed toenails, and tape the hot spot as soon as you feel it.", "Protect a small blister, under about 5 mm, with a ring-shaped pad. A larger one can be drained at its lower edge with a sterile needle, leaving the skin in place, then cleaned and covered. People with diabetes should not drain blisters.", "Stop the burning, take off smouldering clothing and jewellery, and cool the burn with clean water; for pain this can go on for up to about half an hour, but watch that the person does not get chilled. Cover it loosely with a moist, clean dressing and do not pop blisters.", "Get medical help for a blistered burn larger than the casualty's palm, which is about 1% of the body, or for any burn to the face, hands, feet or genitals, one that goes right round a limb, or one with signs in the airway such as singed nose hair or a hoarse voice.", "For a twisted ankle, rest it, apply cold for 10 minutes on and 10 off over the first 24 to 48 hours (never ice straight on the skin), wrap it snugly and raise it."]}]}], "origin": "discovery", "detail": {"avoided": ["Aerie boil times say 1 minute below 6,000 ft and 3 minutes above 3,000 ft, which is internally inconsistent; the course follows CDC (1 minute; 3 minutes above 6,500 ft). Avoid Aerie's figure. Aerie also says iodine should not be used extensively for more than 2 months; CDC (alrea", "Aerie (US) says monitor a tick bite for up to two weeks and suggests permethrin on clothing; UKHSA says watch for several weeks and names DEET. UK source wins; teach UKHSA."], "where": [{"text": "auto: module 6, lesson Minor injuries and heat: blisters, burns, sprains and stings", "n": 6}, {"text": "auto: module 3, lesson A warm night, and spotting hypothermia", "n": 3}, {"text": "module 6: callout reworded; new lesson 'Minor injuries and heat: blisters, burns, sprains and stings'", "n": 1}, {"text": "auto: module 4, lesson Stoves and fire: the safe way to cook", "n": 1}, {"text": "auto: module 4, lesson Making water safe", "n": 1}, {"text": "auto: module 5, lesson How long will it take? Naismith's rule and the weather limits", "n": 1}, {"text": "auto: module 6, lesson Calling for help", "n": 1}, {"text": "module 6: new lesson opens 'It does not replace a first-aid course'; callout says cuts, breaks and head injuries need hands-on training; 999 lines cite the UK source (mrew)", "n": 1}], "skipped": []}}, {"key": "cc-gov", "name": "The Countryside Code: advice for countryside visitors (GOV.UK)", "by": "", "words": 1850, "status": "full", "read": 5, "skipped": [], "sections": 5, "pct": 100, "unread": [], "findings": 7, "verdicts": {"refined": 1, "extended": 4, "confirmed": 2}, "review": "complete", "modules": [1, 5, 6], "note": "", "prov": {"added": "2026-11-19", "source_file": "The Countryside Code England and Wales (GOV.UK).md", "found_by": "unknown"}, "states": {"applied": 5, "confirmed": 2}, "uses": [{"module": 1, "title": "Where you may camp, and leaving no trace", "lessons": [{"title": "Who owns the ground: access rules across the UK and Ireland", "chapters": ["Enjoy the outdoors (ch-004.txt)"], "points": ["The Countryside Code asks you to follow local signs and keep to marked paths unless wider access exists, such as open access land. It lists camping among the activities that may need the landowner's permission."]}, {"title": "Fire, toilet, water and waste in the countryside", "chapters": ["Protect the environment (ch-003.txt)"], "points": ["The Countryside Code notes that some land managers burn heath and moor on purpose between 1 October and 15 April. If you see a fire with nobody tending it, call 999."]}]}, {"module": 5, "title": "Navigation, timing and weather limits", "lessons": [{"title": "Reading a map: scale, symbols and distance", "chapters": ["Signs and symbols (ch-005.txt)"], "points": ["Waymarks on the ground tell you who may use a route in England and Wales: a yellow arrow is a footpath, blue a bridleway, purple a restricted byway and red a byway open to all traffic. An acorn marks a National Trail, and a brown circle with a walker over hills marks open access land, where you may leave the path."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Safety, illness and calling for help", "lessons": [{"title": "Plan like a safe camper: gear, weather and knowledge", "chapters": ["Enjoy the outdoors (ch-004.txt)"], "points": ["The Countryside Code gives the reason: in rural areas you may not see anyone for hours, and phone signal is unreliable in many places. You are responsible for your own safety and for anyone in your care."]}]}], "origin": "", "detail": {"avoided": [], "where": [{"text": "auto: module 1, lesson Who owns the ground: access rules across the UK and Ireland", "n": 1}, {"text": "auto: module 1, lesson Fire, toilet, water and waste in the countryside", "n": 1}, {"text": "auto: module 6, lesson Plan like a safe camper: gear, weather and knowledge", "n": 1}, {"text": "auto: module 5, lesson Reading a map: scale, symbols and distance", "n": 1}, {"text": "module 6, Hazard and control table (livestock row, plus new Scottish cattle row)", "n": 1}], "skipped": []}}, {"key": "cdc-yb-water", "name": "CDC Yellow Book 2026: Water Disinfection for Travelers", "by": "", "words": 3032, "status": "full", "read": 5, "skipped": [], "sections": 5, "pct": 100, "unread": [], "findings": 6, "verdicts": {"refined": 2, "extended": 3, "note": 1}, "review": "complete", "modules": [4], "note": "", "prov": {"added": "2026-11-19", "source_file": "CDC Yellow Book 2026 - Water Disinfection for Travelers.md", "found_by": "unknown"}, "states": {"applied": 6}, "uses": [{"module": 4, "title": "The camp kitchen: stoves, water, food", "lessons": [{"title": "Making water safe", "chapters": ["Field techniques (ch-003.txt)", "Choosing a technique (ch-004.txt)"], "points": ["The CDC travel-health chapter explains why a minute is plenty: every organism that causes illness from water dies within seconds at boiling, and even at about 4,900 m water still boils at about 83°C, well above what kills gut germs. If fuel is short, bring the water to the first simmer, take it off the heat and keep it covered for 30 minutes.", "Most pocket filters are microfilters, with holes under 1 micron. They catch bacteria and the parasites Giardia and Cryptosporidium, but not viruses such as norovirus. Only an ultrafilter, around 0.01 micron, also stops viruses. Makers test their own 'purifier' claims; nobody checks them independently.", "Chlorine and iodine kill bacteria and viruses, and Giardia with a longer wait, but barely touch Cryptosporidium. Chlorine dioxide does kill it, though it can take several hours. Cloudy water weakens every chemical, so let it settle or strain it first. Avoid iodine for more than a few weeks, in pregnancy, or with thyroid problems.", "Match the method to the source. Water with little human or animal activity above it can be treated with any one method. Clear water below grazing cattle or a village needs boiling, UV, a microfilter plus chlorine, or an ultrafilter. Cloudy water needs settling first, then a second step. Practise with your kit before the trip.", "Water near human or animal activity needs a microfilter plus a chemical, or heat, UV or an ultrafilter; chlorine alone misses Cryptosporidium and a microfilter alone misses viruses."]}]}], "origin": "", "detail": {"avoided": [], "where": [{"text": "auto: module 4, lesson Making water safe", "n": 5}, {"text": "module 4, lesson Making water safe (clear does not mean safe; source-based choice)", "n": 1}], "skipped": []}}, {"key": "os-mapbook", "name": "Map reading made easy (Ordnance Survey)", "by": "", "words": 2625, "status": "full", "read": 1, "skipped": [], "sections": 1, "pct": 100, "unread": [], "findings": 4, "verdicts": {"confirmed": 1, "refined": 1, "extended": 2}, "review": "complete", "modules": [5], "note": "", "prov": {"added": "2026-11-19", "source_file": "OS Map reading made easy.pdf", "found_by": "unknown"}, "states": {"confirmed": 1, "applied": 3}, "uses": [{"module": 5, "title": "Navigation, timing and weather limits", "lessons": [{"title": "Reading a map: scale, symbols and distance", "chapters": ["Pages 1 to 16 (ch-001.txt)"], "points": ["A quick estimate: count the 1 km squares you cross. Ordnance Survey puts a diagonal crossing at about 1½ km, and a straight crossing at about 20 minutes at 3 km an hour.", "Brown contour lines join points at the same height above sea level. On a 1:25,000 map they are usually 5 metres apart in height, or 10 metres in mountains. Lines bunched close together mean a steep slope; lines spread apart mean gentle ground, which is what you want for a pitch.", "Contours close together mean a steep slope; spread out, a gentle one."]}]}], "origin": "", "detail": {"avoided": [], "where": [{"text": "module 5, lesson Reading a map: diagonal estimate now the OS figure of about 1.5 km", "n": 1}, {"text": "auto: module 5, lesson Reading a map: scale, symbols and distance", "n": 1}, {"text": "module 5, lesson Grid references: the worked example already adds a nearby landmark", "n": 1}], "skipped": []}}, {"key": "soac-2005", "name": "Scottish Outdoor Access Code (NatureScot, 2005)", "by": "", "words": 31415, "status": "full", "read": 7, "skipped": [], "sections": 7, "pct": 100, "unread": [], "findings": 20, "verdicts": {"extended": 15, "note": 2, "confirmed": 1, "refined": 2}, "review": "complete", "modules": [1, 4, 6], "note": "", "prov": {"added": "2026-11-19", "source_file": "Scottish Outdoor Access Code (NatureScot, 2005).pdf", "found_by": "unknown"}, "states": {"applied": 17, "skipped": 2, "confirmed": 1}, "uses": [{"module": 1, "title": "Where you may camp, and leaving no trace", "lessons": [{"title": "Who owns the ground: access rules across the UK and Ireland", "chapters": ["Pages 1 to 20 (ch-001.txt)", "Pages 21 to 40 (ch-002.txt)"], "points": ["Those rights come from the Land Reform (Scotland) Act 2003 and exist only while you act responsibly. They apply by day or night, and the Scottish Code names wild camping as one of the recreational purposes they cover.", "Some ground is always off limits: land around a house, caravan or tent with enough space for the occupants' privacy, farm buildings and yards, fields where crops are sown or growing, sports pitches in use, school grounds, places that charge for entry, and building sites, railways and quarries. Motorised recreation is not covered, so a car or van stay is outside the rights.", "Because a tent counts as a private place, the same privacy rule protects other campers: do not pitch on top of someone else's camp."]}, {"title": "Fire, toilet, water and waste in the countryside", "chapters": ["Pages 101 to 120 (ch-006.txt)", "Pages 121 to 135 (ch-007.txt)"], "points": ["The Scottish Code is the most detailed on fire. Use a stove rather than an open fire wherever possible. If you do light one, keep it small, under control and supervised, because you might be liable for any damage if it escapes. Never light one in prolonged dry periods, or in forests, woods, farmland, on peaty ground, near buildings or on historic sites, and remove every trace before you leave.", "Scots law backs this up: lighting a fire on enclosed or cultivated land, near a private road or in or near a plantation without the owner's consent is an offence."]}, {"title": "Choosing your first site", "chapters": ["Pages 101 to 120 (ch-006.txt)", "Pages 41 to 60 (ch-003.txt)", "Pages 21 to 40 (ch-002.txt)"], "points": ["The Code's own wild-camping rules for Scotland: keep it lightweight, in small numbers, for two or three nights in one place; do not camp in enclosed fields of crops or farm animals; keep well away from buildings, roads and historic structures; and ask permission to camp near a house.", "Some ground is easily damaged: riverbanks, loch shores, marshes, bogs, mountain tops, steep slopes and coastal dunes. Never camp or light a fire on a historic site, which may look like nothing more than a grassy mound, a line of stones or a ruined wall.", "The Code says to avoid enclosed fields of animals, keep well away from buildings, roads and historic structures, and it does not cover car stays or gardens.", "Cow and sheep droppings can carry E. coli. The risk is small, but it is highest when people camp or picnic where farm animals graze, so a grazed field is a poor pitch."]}]}, {"module": 4, "title": "The camp kitchen: stoves, water, food", "lessons": [{"title": "Food, hygiene and keeping it cold", "chapters": ["Pages 101 to 120 (ch-006.txt)"], "points": ["The Scottish Code adds a site rule: avoid eating in fields where farm animals are, or have recently been, or that have just been spread with lime or slurry, and take every food scrap and wrapper away, because animals may eat them and spread disease."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Safety, illness and calling for help", "lessons": [{"title": "Plan like a safe camper: gear, weather and knowledge", "chapters": ["Pages 21 to 40 (ch-002.txt)", "Pages 81 to 100 (ch-005.txt)"], "points": ["The Scottish Code puts the principle plainly: the outdoors cannot be made risk-free, and a person taking access is generally held to have accepted its obvious risks. Take special care for any children with you.", "In Scotland, check the season. Stag stalking usually runs from 1 July to 20 October, mostly from August, and not normally on Sundays; grouse shooting runs from 12 August to 10 December. Estates post signs and, in some areas, phone lines with the day's routes, so check and follow the suggested alternative."]}, {"title": "Hazards on site and on the walk", "chapters": ["Pages 21 to 40 (ch-002.txt)", "Pages 101 to 120 (ch-006.txt)", "Pages 41 to 60 (ch-003.txt)"], "points": ["Cows can react aggressively, especially with calves or a dog; bulls guard the herd; pigs can turn aggressive at any time.", "Use a neighbouring field if a bull or pigs are present; with cattle, keep a dog on a short lead and let it go if the cows come at you.", "Red flags by day and red lamps at night mark live firing areas, which may not be fenced.", "Do not enter when they show, never pick up objects, and turn back if in doubt.", "In Scotland the Code adds: never take a dog into a field with lambs, calves or other young animals, and keep it on a short lead (two metres) or at heel in ground-nesting areas such as moorland, forest, grassland and loch shores, usually from April to July."]}]}], "origin": "", "detail": {"avoided": [], "where": [{"text": "auto: module 1, lesson Who owns the ground: access rules across the UK and Ireland", "n": 3}, {"text": "auto: module 6, lesson Hazards on site and on the walk", "n": 3}, {"text": "module 6, lessons Plan like a safe camper (outdoors not risk-free, obvious risks accepted) and Hazards on site (land work, gates, bales)", "n": 2}, {"text": "auto: module 1, lesson Fire, toilet, water and waste in the countryside", "n": 2}, {"text": "module 1, lesson Choosing your first site (E. coli where farm animals graze)", "n": 1}, {"text": "module 1, lesson Who owns the ground (a tent counts as a private place; privacy rule protects other campers)", "n": 1}, {"text": "auto: module 1, lesson Choosing your first site", "n": 1}, {"text": "auto: module 6, lesson Plan like a safe camper: gear, weather and knowledge", "n": 1}, {"text": "module 1, lesson Fire, toilet, water and waste: the shallow hole with turf replaced and the 30 m rule were already taught; confirmed against the full Code", "n": 1}, {"text": "auto: module 4, lesson Food, hygiene and keeping it cold", "n": 1}], "skipped": [{"text": "Groups, events and businesses: outside a first small-party trip; the capstone is for a small party", "n": 1}, {"text": "Dealing with other people's irresponsible behaviour is advice for land managers and residents, not for a first-time camper", "n": 1}]}}, {"key": "ukhsa-ticks", "name": "Be Tick Aware toolkit (UK Health Security Agency, 2023)", "by": "UKHSA", "words": 7542, "status": "full", "read": 2, "skipped": [], "sections": 2, "pct": 100, "unread": [], "findings": 7, "verdicts": {"contradicted": 1, "extended": 5, "note": 1}, "review": "complete", "modules": [], "note": "", "prov": {"added": "2026-11-19", "source_file": "UKHSA Be Tick Aware toolkit 2023.pdf", "found_by": "source-discovery", "url": "https://assets.publishing.service.gov.uk/media/642e966bfbe620000f17ddbe/Be_tick_aware_toolkit.pdf", "query": "Source Discovery, 19/11/2026"}, "states": {"applied": 6, "avoided": 1}, "uses": [{"module": 2, "title": "Shelter: choosing, pitching, staying dry", "lessons": [{"title": "Choosing the pitch: wind, water and ground", "chapters": ["Pages 1 to 20 (ch-001.txt)", "Pages 21 to 26 (ch-002.txt)"], "points": ["Ticks are part of pitch choice. They live in dense ground vegetation such as woodland, grassland, moorland and heath, and they are often thick along woodland edges and scrub used by wildlife.", "They do not fly or jump: they wait on the tips of plants and climb on as you brush past, so a short-grass pitch away from bracken and long grass lowers the risk."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Safety, illness and calling for help", "lessons": [{"title": "Recognising illness: cold, gas and dirty water", "chapters": ["Pages 1 to 20 (ch-001.txt)", "Pages 21 to 26 (ch-002.txt)"], "points": ["Lyme disease is the most common tick-borne infection in the UK, and the tick that bites people most is Ixodes ricinus, the sheep or deer tick.", "About 1,500 laboratory-confirmed cases are reported a year in England and Wales.", "Young ticks, called nymphs, matter most because they are hard to spot and stay on longer; their activity peaks from April to June, but bites happen in any month.", "Cut the risk: keep to clear paths, wear long trousers, long sleeves and light colours so ticks show up, and use a repellent such as DEET as the maker directs. Each evening, look and feel for ticks in skin folds, armpits, groin, waistband, behind the knees, the back of the neck and the hairline; children are often bitten on the head and neck.", "To remove a tick, use fine-tipped tweezers or a tick tool, grip it as close to the skin as you can and pull upwards slowly and firmly. Then apply antiseptic or wash with soap and water, and watch the spot for several weeks."]}]}], "origin": "discovery", "detail": {"avoided": ["Internal inconsistency in the toolkit: 'approximately 20' tick species (executive summary) vs '22' (tick facts), and 3 active stages vs 4 stages including eggs. Avoid stating a species count."], "where": [{"text": "auto: module 6, lesson Recognising illness: cold, gas and dirty water", "n": 4}, {"text": "module 6, lesson Recognising illness: tick callout replaced by 'Ticks and Lyme disease' section (no species count, no 3,000 to 4,000 estimate)", "n": 1}, {"text": "auto: module 2, lesson Choosing the pitch: wind, water and ground", "n": 1}], "skipped": []}}, {"key": "uksc-dartmoor", "name": "Darwall v Dartmoor National Park Authority [2025] UKSC 20", "by": "United Kingdom Supreme Court (UKSC)", "words": 9407, "status": "full", "read": 2, "skipped": [], "sections": 2, "pct": 100, "unread": [], "findings": 6, "verdicts": {"extended": 3, "confirmed": 2, "note": 1}, "review": "complete", "modules": [], "note": "", "prov": {"added": "2026-11-19", "source_file": "Darwall v Dartmoor NPA 2025 UKSC 20 judgment.pdf", "found_by": "source-discovery", "url": "https://supremecourt.uk/uploads/uksc_2023_0126_judgment_03167171c8.pdf", "query": "Source Discovery, 19/11/2026"}, "states": {"applied": 4, "confirmed": 2}, "uses": [{"module": 1, "title": "Where you may camp, and leaving no trace", "lessons": [{"title": "Who owns the ground: access rules across the UK and Ireland", "chapters": ["Pages 1 to 20 (ch-001.txt)", "Pages 21 to 24 (ch-002.txt)"], "points": ["Dartmoor's allowance is a legal right, not a favour. Section 10(1) of the Dartmoor Commons Act 1985 gives the public a right of access to the Commons on foot and on horseback for open-air recreation, and in [2025] UKSC 20 the UK's top court held unanimously that this includes wild camping.", "The right holds only while you keep the byelaws: no vehicle, caravan or structure other than a tent, and no camping in the areas the byelaws list, which is why the map matters. Break them and the landowner can treat you as a trespasser.", "The Commons are privately owned moorland where local commoners graze their animals, about 37% of the National Park and 75% of its moorland, so you camp as a guest on working land.", "The case went one way in the High Court and the other on appeal before the final ruling, so check the date on any guidance you read.", "An access right does not always include camping. An older law giving the public access to metropolitan commons for air and exercise excluded any right to camp or light a fire, so read what each right actually covers."]}]}], "origin": "discovery", "detail": {"avoided": [], "where": [{"text": "auto: module 1, lesson Who owns the ground: access rules across the UK and Ireland", "n": 4}], "skipped": []}}], "pages": [{"key": "soac", "status": "unknown", "name": "Being a cool camper", "by": "NatureScot, Scottish Outdoor Access Code practical guide, 2025", "modules": [1, 2, 4, 6, "cap"], "note": "Camping", "uses": [{"module": 1, "title": "Where you may camp, and leaving no trace", "lessons": [{"title": "Who owns the ground: access rules across the UK and Ireland", "chapters": ["Being a cool camper (camping)"], "points": ["Scotland is the exception inside the UK. There you can enjoy the outdoors on most land if you act responsibly, and that includes lightweight camping in a tent, in small numbers, for no more than two or three nights in one place.", "Lightweight camping in small tents, small numbers, two or three nights in one place, following the Scottish Outdoor Access Code. Seasonal byelaws limit camping in parts of Loch Lomond and the Trossachs National Park.", "Spot C is a lay-by in the Highlands where you plan to sleep in your car. Scottish access rights do not include motor vehicles, so parking must respect any owner restrictions.", "The Scottish guide says exactly that, and adds that access rights do not include motor vehicles."]}, {"title": "Fire, toilet, water and waste in the countryside", "chapters": ["Being a cool camper (camping)"], "points": ["Four jobs cause most camping damage: fires, toileting, washing and rubbish. The Scottish guide warns that open fires can quickly get out of control and that wildfires devastate wildlife and communities.", "Do not light an open fire, including in fire bowls or barbecues, in prolonged dry weather or near forests, farmland, peaty ground, buildings or historic sites.", "Use a camping stove on a level, non-flammable surface away from dry grass and your tent, and keep watch.", "Pick up broken glass and cigarette ends, because glass can focus sun and ends can ignite dry grass.", "Leave deadwood on the ground, because it is a home and food for many creatures."]}, {"title": "Choosing your first site", "chapters": ["Being a cool camper (camping)"], "points": ["The Scottish guidance agrees in spirit: managed campsites with facilities can be a much better option in busy areas.", "For anything else, camp well away from buildings and roads, use a small tent, avoid fields with crops or animals, and keep a backup plan for when it is busy."]}]}, {"module": 2, "title": "Shelter: choosing, pitching, staying dry", "lessons": [{"title": "Choosing the pitch: wind, water and ground", "chapters": ["Being a cool camper (camping)"], "points": ["That is about 61 metres (our conversion). Use the larger of this and any local rule, such as Scotland's 30 m guidance for toileting."]}]}, {"module": 4, "title": "The camp kitchen: stoves, water, food", "lessons": [{"title": "Stoves and fire: the safe way to cook", "chapters": ["Being a cool camper (camping)"], "points": ["Place the stove on a stable, level, non-flammable surface away from dry grass and your tent, and keep a close eye on it."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Safety, illness and calling for help", "lessons": [{"title": "Calling for help", "chapters": ["Being a cool camper (camping)"], "points": ["Call 999 immediately if a fire is out of control."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "The brief and the kit", "chapters": ["Being a cool camper (camping)"], "points": ["Trowel, toilet paper, sanitiser, sealable bags, rubbish bag."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "dartmoor", "status": "unknown", "name": "Camping and the Backpack Camping Code", "by": "Dartmoor National Park Authority", "modules": [1, 6], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 1, "title": "Where you may camp, and leaving no trace", "lessons": [{"title": "Who owns the ground: access rules across the UK and Ireland", "chapters": ["Camping and the Backpack Camping Code"], "points": ["Backpack camping is allowed for one or two nights in some areas of open moorland, shown on an interactive map, without prior landowner approval. No large tents, no groups over six, no vehicle stays.", "Spot B is open moorland on Dartmoor, a 6 km walk from the car park. You check the interactive map: if the spot sits in a permitted area and you are a party of four with two small backpack tents, you may stay one or two nights."]}, {"title": "Fire, toilet, water and waste in the countryside", "chapters": ["Camping and the Backpack Camping Code"], "points": ["Dartmoor's backpack camping code allows no open fires or barbecues at all.", "Dartmoor asks you to bury poo and used paper under at least 15 cm of turf.", "Do not pollute streams or rivers.", "At least 15 cm under turf; take a trowel.", "No open fires or barbecues."]}, {"title": "Choosing your first site", "chapters": ["Camping and the Backpack Camping Code"], "points": ["A first night, families, large tents, vehicles. Large tents and campervans must use designated sites on Dartmoor.", "A quieter, basic pitch with a landowner who knows the area.", "Experienced walkers with a small tent where the rule allows it.", "You carry everything, including the means to bury or remove waste."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Safety, illness and calling for help", "lessons": [{"title": "Hazards on site and on the walk", "chapters": ["Camping and the Backpack Camping Code"], "points": ["Breeding season is 1 March to 31 July.", "Avoid nesting sites and take extra care."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "rei-beg", "status": "unknown", "name": "Camping for Beginners", "by": "REI Co-op Expert Advice", "modules": [1, 3, 4, 6, "cap"], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 1, "title": "Where you may camp, and leaving no trace", "lessons": [{"title": "Fire, toilet, water and waste in the countryside", "chapters": ["Camping for Beginners"], "points": ["Wash dishes with clean water and soap and a scrubber, and keep wash water away from streams."]}, {"title": "Choosing your first site", "chapters": ["Camping for Beginners"], "points": ["For a first trip, REI recommends a developed campground rather than dispersed camping.", "Dispersed camping needs more skill, kit and comfort outdoors, and the guide says most first-time campers are more at ease in a developed campground.", "Look for flush toilets and running water, and check whether tap water is treated.", "Book ahead in busy months.", "Practise pitching the tent at home before the trip, and set up the tent, bag and pad as soon as you arrive so you are not doing it in the dark."]}]}, {"module": 3, "title": "The sleep system and staying warm", "lessons": [{"title": "The pad under you: R-value and building a sleep system", "chapters": ["Camping for Beginners"], "points": ["Big air mattresses look plush but have little insulation.", "REI notes that big air mattresses lack insulation."]}, {"title": "A warm night, and spotting hypothermia", "chapters": ["Camping for Beginners"], "points": ["Set up your tent, bag and pad before dark."]}]}, {"module": 4, "title": "The camp kitchen: stoves, water, food", "lessons": [{"title": "Stoves and fire: the safe way to cook", "chapters": ["Camping for Beginners"], "points": ["A classic two-burner propane camp stove is REI's suggestion for a beginner car camper.", "Test it in the garden before the trip so you know how it works."]}, {"title": "Food, hygiene and keeping it cold", "chapters": ["Camping for Beginners"], "points": ["Freeze as much food as you can to act as ice blocks."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Safety, illness and calling for help", "lessons": [{"title": "Recognising illness: cold, gas and dirty water", "chapters": ["Camping for Beginners"], "points": ["This course is not a first-aid course. The next lesson gives basic care for blisters, burns, sprains, stings and heat, but cuts, breaks and head injuries need hands-on training. REI recommends carrying a first-aid kit and replenishing it after each trip.", "Sun and insects: REI advises planning for sun and bugs with sunscreen and repellent."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "The brief and the kit", "chapters": ["Camping for Beginners"], "points": ["Head torch (a lantern is a bonus).", "Stove, fuel, lighter, pots, plates, scrubber, soap, towel, tubs; water treatment.", "Avoid cotton; warm coat, hat and gloves, sturdy shoes, waterproofs.", "Pack kitchen kit in one lidded box so it is ready for next time.", "Replenish your first-aid kit after every trip."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "lnt-ie", "status": "unknown", "name": "Camping in Ireland", "by": "Leave No Trace Ireland, 2021", "modules": [1, "cap"], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 1, "title": "Where you may camp, and leaving no trace", "lessons": [{"title": "Who owns the ground: access rules across the UK and Ireland", "chapters": ["Camping in Ireland"], "points": ["The first fact of camping in these islands is that the ground belongs to someone. In Ireland, land is owned by either private owners or state bodies.", "There is no provision for free access to land for recreation in Ireland, so access depends on the goodwill of the landowner.", "Permission must be acquired even on public land. National parks and nature reserves each have their own camping policy.", "Spot A is a farm field beside a lane in County Mayo. All land in Ireland is owned, so you must ask the farmer.", "Leave No Trace Ireland says all land is owned and permission must be acquired, even on public land."]}, {"title": "Leave No Trace: seven principles you can apply tomorrow", "chapters": ["Camping in Ireland"], "points": ["Leave No Trace Ireland advises getting permission before you camp in Ireland and treats planning as the most important part of any trip."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "Rubric, run and review", "chapters": ["Camping in Ireland"], "points": []}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "rei-setup", "status": "unknown", "name": "How to Set Up a Tent", "by": "REI Co-op Expert Advice", "modules": [1, 2, 6, "cap"], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 1, "title": "Where you may camp, and leaving no trace", "lessons": [{"title": "Leave No Trace: seven principles you can apply tomorrow", "chapters": ["How to Set Up a Tent"], "points": ["The REI guide also advises focusing activity where vegetation is absent."]}, {"title": "Choosing your first site", "chapters": ["How to Set Up a Tent"], "points": []}]}, {"module": 2, "title": "Shelter: choosing, pitching, staying dry", "lessons": [{"title": "Choosing the pitch: wind, water and ground", "chapters": ["How to Set Up a Tent"], "points": ["Where you pitch matters as much as what you pitch. REI breaks tent setup into pre-trip preparation, site choice, pitching and guylines.", "On site, you want to minimise impact and maximise weather protection.", "Look for natural windbreaks such as trees or a hill upwind.", "Avoid camping near damaged trees or limbs that could fall in a gust.", "Seek higher, drier ground."]}, {"title": "Pitching: order, pegs, fly tension and guylines", "chapters": ["How to Set Up a Tent"], "points": ["Practice at home. REI says the worst time to learn is after a long day, in the dark, in rain.", "Read the instructions, check all the parts, and pack a copy of the instructions.", "Clear sticks and stones from the pitch.", "If it is windy, stake the corners straight away so the tent does not fly like a kite.", "Seat each pole section slowly to avoid chipping or bending."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Safety, illness and calling for help", "lessons": [{"title": "Hazards on site and on the walk", "chapters": ["How to Set Up a Tent"], "points": ["Damaged trees can drop limbs in a gust.", "Do not pitch beneath them.", "Controls: move the tent away from the oak and above the stream's reach, close the gate, keep food sealed, put the torch by the door.", "REI says to avoid camping near damaged trees or limbs that can fall."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "The brief and the kit", "chapters": ["How to Set Up a Tent"], "points": ["Tent with fly, footprint, pegs, spare cord and pegs, instructions."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "brit-muir", "status": "unknown", "name": "John Muir", "by": "Encyclopaedia Britannica", "modules": [1], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 1, "title": "Where you may camp, and leaving no trace", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: John Muir", "chapters": ["John Muir"], "points": ["John Muir was a Scottish-born American naturalist who founded the Sierra Club in 1892 and served as its first president.", "His writing influenced the creation of national parks.", "Muir relied on camp-fires. In the UK and Ireland, fires are often restricted, so follow this module's fire rules and the landowner's rules, not Muir's habits. Britannica also notes that Muir held deeply harmful attitudes toward Black and Indigenous peoples, so he is a source for these ideas, not a model in general."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "sc-menu", "status": "unknown", "name": "John Muir's Menu", "by": "J. Parker Huber, Sierra Club John Muir Exhibit", "modules": [1], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 1, "title": "Where you may camp, and leaving no trace", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: John Muir", "chapters": ["John Muir's Menu"], "points": ["Eat little and carry food that keeps. A Sierra Club article on his trips says he rolled bread and tea in a pair of blankets with some sugar and a tin cup, and that he did not hunt or fish."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "muir-np", "status": "unknown", "name": "Our National Parks", "by": "John Muir, Project Gutenberg #60929", "modules": [1], "note": "1901, full text", "uses": [{"module": 1, "title": "Where you may camp, and leaving no trace", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: John Muir", "chapters": ["Our National Parks (1901), full text"], "points": ["We read his own book Our National Parks (1901) in part, and secondary pages from Britannica and the Sierra Club. His own text makes this record partial to rich for this topic, and the opinions below are his, not ours.", "Travel light so the land decides your route. He wrote that nearly all his mountaineering was done on foot, carrying as little as possible and depending on camp-fires for warmth, so that he might be light and free to go wherever his studies led.", "Add a load only when the trip demands it, and know its cost. For a long Sequoia trip he was persuaded to take a small wild mule to carry provisions and a pair of blankets, so a load was a deliberate choice, not a default.", "Live off the place without damaging it. He describes dead resin-rich roots that make fires that burn through the thickest storms, and fallen needles of old groves that make a fine mattress.", "Treat fire as the greatest risk to the place. He complains that shepherds set innumerable fires that burn the seedlings on which the forest's permanence depends, and many of the old trees too."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "lnt", "status": "unknown", "name": "The 7 Principles", "by": "Leave No Trace Center for Outdoor Ethics", "modules": [1, "cap"], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 1, "title": "Where you may camp, and leaving no trace", "lessons": [{"title": "Leave No Trace: seven principles you can apply tomorrow", "chapters": ["The 7 Principles"], "points": ["Leave No Trace is a set of seven principles for reducing your impact outdoors.", "Know weather, rules and conditions before you go.", "Stick to paths and ground that can recover.", "Pack it in, pack it out, including food scraps.", "Take photos, not souvenirs."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "The brief and the kit", "chapters": ["The 7 Principles"], "points": ["Rules: campsite booked; tent and fire rules read from the site; stove only."]}, {"title": "Rubric, run and review", "chapters": ["The 7 Principles"], "points": ["States the site, whose land it is, the camping rule, and the Leave No Trace actions."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "cc-ni", "status": "unknown", "name": "The Countryside Code", "by": "nidirect, Northern Ireland Executive", "modules": [1, 6], "note": "Northern Ireland", "uses": [{"module": 1, "title": "Where you may camp, and leaving no trace", "lessons": [{"title": "Who owns the ground: access rules across the UK and Ireland", "chapters": ["The Countryside Code (Northern Ireland)"], "points": ["Most land is private property and access is only with the goodwill and tolerance of the owner."]}, {"title": "Leave No Trace: seven principles you can apply tomorrow", "chapters": ["The Countryside Code (Northern Ireland)"], "points": ["The Northern Ireland code adds that you should keep to paths across farmland, leave gates as you find them and avoid fields with animals."]}, {"title": "Fire, toilet, water and waste in the countryside", "chapters": ["The Countryside Code (Northern Ireland)"], "points": []}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Safety, illness and calling for help", "lessons": [{"title": "Plan like a safe camper: gear, weather and knowledge", "chapters": ["The Countryside Code (Northern Ireland)"], "points": ["If possible, do not go alone.", "Wear suitable clothing and footwear because the weather can change quickly, and do not go if conditions are beyond your experience."]}, {"title": "Hazards on site and on the walk", "chapters": ["The Countryside Code (Northern Ireland)"], "points": ["Avoid fields with animals."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "cc-ew", "status": "unknown", "name": "The Countryside Code: advice for countryside visitors", "by": "Natural England and Natural Resources Wales, updated 26/05/2023", "modules": [1, 6, "cap"], "note": "England and Wales", "uses": [{"module": 1, "title": "Where you may camp, and leaving no trace", "lessons": [{"title": "Who owns the ground: access rules across the UK and Ireland", "chapters": ["The Countryside Code: advice for countryside visitors (England and Wales)"], "points": []}, {"title": "Leave No Trace: seven principles you can apply tomorrow", "chapters": ["The Countryside Code: advice for countryside visitors (England and Wales)"], "points": ["The UK codes say the same things in different words. The Countryside Code for England and Wales asks you to take your litter home and leave no trace of your visit."]}, {"title": "Fire, toilet, water and waste in the countryside", "chapters": ["The Countryside Code: advice for countryside visitors (England and Wales)"], "points": ["The England and Wales Countryside Code adds that you should only have barbecues where signs say you can.", "Bring a bag and take rubbish and food waste home.", "Dropping litter and dumping rubbish are criminal offences in England and Wales."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Safety, illness and calling for help", "lessons": [{"title": "Hazards on site and on the walk", "chapters": ["The Countryside Code: advice for countryside visitors (England and Wales)"], "points": ["Behaviour is unpredictable, especially with young.", "Give them plenty of space and do not feed them.", "Fires can devastate wildlife and property.", "Use a stove; only use barbecues where signs allow; call 999 for an unattended fire.", "Conditions change quickly on mountains and coast."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "The brief and the kit", "chapters": ["The Countryside Code: advice for countryside visitors (England and Wales)"], "points": []}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "kephart-wc", "status": "part", "name": "Camping and Woodcraft, full text of the Internet Archive copy", "by": "Horace Kephart, via Internet Archive", "modules": [2, 5, "cap"], "note": "Forest travel, rivers, thirst and cookery sections read", "uses": [{"module": 2, "title": "Shelter: choosing, pitching, staying dry", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Horace Kephart", "chapters": ["Camping and Woodcraft, full text of the Internet Archive copy (forest travel, rivers, thirst and cookery sections read)"], "points": ["Expect slow going. He says that a mile and a half an hour of actual progress is making good time in the woods, because the ground is covered with stubs, loose stones, slippery roots, saplings, downwood and vines.", "Choose the line of travel by the ground, not the shortest map line. He says the aboriginal rule of keeping away from streams and following the ridges between them still holds for a party on foot, because a bank route means many fords, thickets and bogs.", "Plan for a river to rise. He says a heavy rainstorm may send the river out of its banks and leave a party marooned where it can go neither forward nor back.", "Manage thirst and water by habit. Drink as often as you please but not much at a time, and never try to quench thirst by swallowing snow or ice; melt it first in the mouth if no fire is possible.", "A route in rough woodland is 9 km. Kephart's figure is a mile and a half an hour, which is 1.5 × 1.609 = 2.41 km per hour."]}]}, {"module": 5, "title": "Navigation, timing and weather limits", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Ernest Shackleton", "chapters": ["Camping and Woodcraft, full text of the Internet Archive copy (forest travel, rivers, thirst and cookery sections read)"], "points": ["The camp is 8 km away and you need to be back by dark at 18:00. Your pace in rough country is 2.4 km per hour (Kephart's 1.5 miles)."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Ernest Shackleton", "chapters": ["Camping and Woodcraft, full text of the Internet Archive copy (forest travel, rivers, thirst and cookery sections read)"], "points": ["Plan the walk at 2.4 km per hour for 12 km, which is 5 hours. Add 30 percent for rough ground and mistakes: 6.5 hours."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "bmc", "status": "unknown", "name": "Camping stove safety: how to avoid carbon monoxide and fire risks", "by": "Spenser Gray, British Mountaineering Council", "modules": [2, 4, 6, "cap"], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 2, "title": "Shelter: choosing, pitching, staying dry", "lessons": [{"title": "Staying dry inside: condensation", "chapters": ["Camping stove safety: how to avoid carbon monoxide and fire risks"], "points": ["Never fix a cold tent by bringing a stove or barbecue inside. That creates carbon monoxide and fire risk. See Module 4."]}]}, {"module": 4, "title": "The camp kitchen: stoves, water, food", "lessons": [{"title": "Stoves and fire: the safe way to cook", "chapters": ["Camping stove safety: how to avoid carbon monoxide and fire risks"], "points": ["Camp stoves can also cause fire, trapped escape routes, carbon monoxide poisoning and burns, according to the British Mountaineering Council.", "Inspect for damage such as loose fixings, pan supports or perished O-rings.", "Close the valve before attaching the canister and do not force the threads.", "Check the connection is secure and not cross-threaded.", "In a ventilated space, briefly turn on the gas to check it is only coming from the burner, then let any escaped gas disperse before lighting."]}, {"title": "Carbon monoxide: the silent hazard", "chapters": ["Camping stove safety: how to avoid carbon monoxide and fire risks"], "points": ["Any stove that burns fuel produces some.", "Light-headedness, confusion, forgetfulness, fatigue after using a stove in an enclosed space.", "Turn off the stove, ventilate, move to fresh air; seek medical help if symptoms persist.", "Keep ventilation and a clear exit if you must cook indoors."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Safety, illness and calling for help", "lessons": [{"title": "Recognising illness: cold, gas and dirty water", "chapters": ["Camping stove safety: how to avoid carbon monoxide and fire risks"], "points": ["The pattern fits carbon monoxide: headaches, dizziness and nausea after a stove in an enclosed space.", "Symptoms after using a stove in an enclosed space point to carbon monoxide."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "The brief and the kit", "chapters": ["Camping stove safety: how to avoid carbon monoxide and fire risks"], "points": []}, {"title": "Rubric, run and review", "chapters": ["Camping stove safety: how to avoid carbon monoxide and fire risks"], "points": ["Stove, carbon monoxide rule, water treatment and food-temperature plan all present."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "nid-co", "status": "unknown", "name": "Carbon monoxide dangers if camping, caravanning or boating", "by": "nidirect, published 02/04/2026", "modules": [2, 4, 6, "cap"], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 2, "title": "Shelter: choosing, pitching, staying dry", "lessons": [{"title": "Staying dry inside: condensation", "chapters": ["Carbon monoxide dangers if camping, caravanning or boating"], "points": []}]}, {"module": 4, "title": "The camp kitchen: stoves, water, food", "lessons": [{"title": "Stoves and fire: the safe way to cook", "chapters": ["Carbon monoxide dangers if camping, caravanning or boating"], "points": ["Linked to carbon monoxide deaths in tents.", "Never take a smouldering or lit barbecue into a tent, even after cooking."]}, {"title": "Carbon monoxide: the silent hazard", "chapters": ["Carbon monoxide dangers if camping, caravanning or boating"], "points": ["Carbon monoxide is a colourless, tasteless, odourless, non-irritating gas.", "It can build up to deadly levels in enclosed spaces, including tent awnings.", "Headache, dizziness, nausea and vomiting, breathlessness, exhaustion, drowsiness, light-headedness, collapse.", "Open doors and windows and go outside into fresh air.", "Never take a gas stove, light or heater into a tent unless it is a permanent fixture installed and maintained correctly."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Safety, illness and calling for help", "lessons": [{"title": "Recognising illness: cold, gas and dirty water", "chapters": ["Carbon monoxide dangers if camping, caravanning or boating"], "points": ["Headache, dizziness, nausea, drowsiness, collapse.", "Ventilate, get outside, seek urgent help if needed.", "The signs of carbon monoxide poisoning are often mistaken for flu or food poisoning.", "Action: get everyone into fresh air, turn off the stove and ventilate. Seek medical help; call 999 if anyone is drowsy or collapses."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "The brief and the kit", "chapters": ["Carbon monoxide dangers if camping, caravanning or boating"], "points": ["A lit stove or barbecue creates carbon monoxide and fire risk."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "rei-tent", "status": "unknown", "name": "How to Choose a Camping Tent", "by": "Kelly Bastone, REI Co-op Expert Advice, updated 07/10/2025", "modules": [2, "cap"], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 2, "title": "Shelter: choosing, pitching, staying dry", "lessons": [{"title": "Choosing a tent: size, season and shape", "chapters": ["How to Choose a Camping Tent"], "points": ["Tents are sold by the number of people they sleep, and that rating roughly equals how many sleeping pads fit on the floor.", "Many people prefer a tent at least one size larger than the number sharing it.", "Space for cards, changing clothes or gear is not included in the person rating.", "Most campers are best served by a three-season tent.", "It typically uses a double wall: a mostly mesh inner for ventilation and insects, and a waterproof fly for rain."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "Rubric, run and review", "chapters": ["How to Choose a Camping Tent"], "points": ["Tent size and pitch reasoned; bag and pad ratings matched to the forecast for the pitch."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "rei-cond", "status": "unknown", "name": "How to Prevent Condensation in a Tent", "by": "Ken Knapp, REI Co-op Expert Advice", "modules": [2, "cap"], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 2, "title": "Shelter: choosing, pitching, staying dry", "lessons": [{"title": "Choosing the pitch: wind, water and ground", "chapters": ["How to Prevent Condensation in a Tent"], "points": ["Warmer air under trees means a warmer fly and less condensation."]}, {"title": "Staying dry inside: condensation", "chapters": ["How to Prevent Condensation in a Tent"], "points": ["Condensation forms when warm air meets a cold surface, like the outside of a cold glass. In a tent, warm air moves to the cooler fly and condenses on the underside, then drips or soaks the fabric.", "REI boils the answer down to three strategies: pitch well, reduce moisture sources inside, and ventilate.", "Dry ground, preferably under trees.", "Dry wet things outside during the day; never let a wet dog sleep in the tent.", "Open fly doors, vents and inner windows; keep the fly taut; pitch the door into a gentle breeze."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "Rubric, run and review", "chapters": ["How to Prevent Condensation in a Tent"], "points": ["What to change: the fly dripped by morning, so next time pitch under trees and open the vents earlier."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "kephart10", "status": "part", "name": "The Book of Camping and Woodcraft", "by": "Horace Kephart, Outing Publishing Company, via Internet Archive", "modules": [2], "note": "1910 edition), chapter VII The Camp (read in an earlier pass; not re-checked in the second pass", "uses": [{"module": 2, "title": "Shelter: choosing, pitching, staying dry", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Horace Kephart", "chapters": ["The Book of Camping and Woodcraft (1910 edition), chapter VII The Camp (read in an earlier pass; not re-checked in the second pass)"], "points": ["Horace Kephart published The Book of Camping and Woodcraft in 1906, and later editions followed.", "We read two parts of the Internet Archive copy. The pitch checklist comes from the chapter read in an earlier pass and has not been re-checked in this pass. The pace, river and thirst advice was read in this pass from the full text. The record is partial to rich.", "Choose the pitch for drainage and exposure. His camp chapter looks for an open spot that is level enough for the tent and fire but raised so it drains, well above any chance of overflow from a stream, with exposure to the breeze in summer and shelter from the prevailing wind in cold weather.", "Look overhead. He warns against pitching under a tree that would drip, near a dead tree, or among shallow-rooted trees a storm may overthrow, and he says to clear brush, stubs and rotten wood from the site.", "Kephart wrote for American forests in the early 1900s. He also advises trenching around the tent, which is poor practice on UK and Irish sites and forbidden in many places. Choose a slightly raised pitch instead. His advice about dead trees applies anywhere."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "nansen-fn", "status": "part", "name": "Farthest North, Vol. II", "by": "Fridtjof Nansen, Project Gutenberg #34120", "modules": [3, 4], "note": "1897), full text (the sledge-journey equipment chapter read", "uses": [{"module": 3, "title": "The sleep system and staying warm", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Fridtjof Nansen", "chapters": ["Farthest North, Vol. II (1897), full text (the sledge-journey equipment chapter read)"], "points": ["We read his own account of the equipment for the 1895 journey in Farthest North, Volume II (read in the equipment and preparation chapter), and a Royal Scottish Geographical Society article and Wikipedia. His own text makes the record rich for this topic.", "Test the clothes in the real cold. On 29 November he walked in his thick wolfskin dress at -37.6 °C and sweated, and on 9 December at -41 °C the perspiration still poured down his back. He concluded the dress was too warm (the temperatures and sweating are unverified: not found in the sources checked).", "Try the sleeping bag and change the design. One-man bags of reindeer calf skin proved insufficiently warm, so he went back to a double bag of adult reindeer skin, in which the occupants warm each other.", "Count the weight. He says a bag for two men is not a little lighter than two single bags, and says the objection that a joint bag disturbs sleep was not true in his experience.", "Take a tent, even a thin one. He says a tent gives so much protection and comfort that its small extra weight is worth carrying. He tried a floored silk tent of about 7 pounds but dropped it, because snow carried in on the feet melted from body heat and the floor absorbed the water, making the tent heavier."]}]}, {"module": 4, "title": "The camp kitchen: stoves, water, food", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Fridtjof Nansen", "chapters": ["Farthest North, Vol. II (1897), full text (the sledge-journey equipment chapter read)"], "points": ["We read his own account of the cooking apparatus in Farthest North, Volume II, the Greenland crossing material at secondary level, and a schools resource on the cooker. The cooker facts below are from his own book, so the record is rich for this topic.", "Choose the fuel by heat for weight. He chose petroleum over alcohol, because alcohol, though easy to burn, does not give so much heat for its weight as petroleum when the latter is entirely consumed, as in the lamp he used.", "Burn it completely. The lamp, the Primus, turns petroleum to gas before burning it, which makes combustion unusually complete. Professor Torup's laboratory experiments gave the cooker 90 to 93 per cent of the heat the petroleum should theoretically yield.", "Make one flame do several jobs. The apparatus had two boilers and a vessel for melting snow or ice, so food could be cooked and water melted at once, and in the morning and evening there was as much to drink as wished.", "Make it light. The cooker, with two tin mugs, a ladle and two spoons, weighed exactly 8 pounds 13 ounces, and the Primus lamp 4½ ounces."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "wiki-nansen", "status": "unknown", "name": "Fridtjof Nansen", "by": "Wikipedia", "modules": [3], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 3, "title": "The sleep system and staying warm", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Fridtjof Nansen", "chapters": ["Fridtjof Nansen"], "points": ["Wikipedia credits him with the layer principle, in which heavy garments gave way to layers of lightweight material."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "rsgs-nansen", "status": "unknown", "name": "Fridtjof Nansen: The First Crossing of Greenland", "by": "Royal Scottish Geographical Society", "modules": [3], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 3, "title": "The sleep system and staying warm", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Fridtjof Nansen", "chapters": ["Fridtjof Nansen: The First Crossing of Greenland"], "points": ["Fridtjof Nansen led the first crossing of Greenland in 1888, and in 1895 made a long sledge journey from the ship Fram.", "Choose layers over bulk. The Greenland crossing party used light woollen garments, and he is described as a pioneer of the layering system.", "In a storm on the Greenland crossing, his party lay in the tent in their sleeping bags for three days while the ice melted under them and rain poured above.", "Layers beat bulk, and a spare dry set matters.", "What is under you matters as much as what is over you."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "rei-sleep", "status": "unknown", "name": "How to Sleep Well While Camping", "by": "REI Co-op Expert Advice", "modules": [3, 4, 6, "cap"], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 3, "title": "The sleep system and staying warm", "lessons": [{"title": "Sleeping bags and what the numbers mean", "chapters": ["How to Sleep Well While Camping"], "points": ["Style matters too. Car-camping bags are cut wider for comfort but hold heat less efficiently. Mummy bags fit closer and are warmer for their weight.", "For milder nights, a rectangular bag can unzip to act like a quilt."]}, {"title": "The pad under you: R-value and building a sleep system", "chapters": ["How to Sleep Well While Camping"], "points": ["To add warmth, put a closed-cell foam pad under your regular pad."]}, {"title": "A warm night, and spotting hypothermia", "chapters": ["How to Sleep Well While Camping"], "points": ["Change into clean, dry layers and socks.", "Do not overdress. Bulky clothing in a bag can reduce its ability to trap heat; drape a jacket over the bag instead.", "Eat a snack and drink a warm, non-alcoholic drink. Alcohol widens blood vessels and speeds heat loss.", "Wear a warm hat and cinch the bag hood.", "Fill empty space in the bag with dry clothes, and keep a warm water bottle near your core."]}]}, {"module": 4, "title": "The camp kitchen: stoves, water, food", "lessons": [{"title": "Carbon monoxide: the silent hazard", "chapters": ["How to Sleep Well While Camping"], "points": ["Better: put on dry layers, eat hot food cooked outside under a tarp, and use a warm water bottle in the bag."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Safety, illness and calling for help", "lessons": [{"title": "Hazards on site and on the walk", "chapters": ["How to Sleep Well While Camping"], "points": ["It is darker outdoors than most people expect.", "Wear a head torch and set up before dark."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "The brief and the kit", "chapters": ["How to Sleep Well While Camping"], "points": []}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "nhs-hypo", "status": "unknown", "name": "Hypothermia", "by": "NHS, reviewed 09/06/2023", "modules": [3, 6], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 3, "title": "The sleep system and staying warm", "lessons": [{"title": "How you lose heat: wind, height and wet", "chapters": ["Hypothermia"], "points": ["Wet clothes and wet ground make you cold."]}, {"title": "A warm night, and spotting hypothermia", "chapters": ["Hypothermia"], "points": ["Hypothermia is a dangerous drop in body temperature below 35 °C, against a normal 37 °C. It is a medical emergency that needs hospital treatment.", "Symptoms include shivering, pale, cold and dry skin, slurred speech, slow breathing, tiredness or confusion.", "Causes include too few clothes in the cold, staying out too long, falling into cold water, and wet clothes.", "Move the person somewhere sheltered as quickly as possible.", "Use a hot bath, hot water bottle or heat lamp."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Safety, illness and calling for help", "lessons": [{"title": "Recognising illness: cold, gas and dirty water", "chapters": ["Hypothermia"], "points": ["Shivering, pale cold skin, slurred speech, slow breathing, tiredness or confusion.", "Shelter, remove wet clothes, wrap with head covered, warm sugary drink if alert, keep them awake, call 999.", "NHS: shelter, wrap, call 999; do not rub limbs or give alcohol."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "mrew", "status": "unknown", "name": "In case of emergency", "by": "Mountain Rescue England and Wales", "modules": [3, 5, 6, "cap"], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 3, "title": "The sleep system and staying warm", "lessons": [{"title": "A warm night, and spotting hypothermia", "chapters": ["In case of emergency"], "points": ["In the hills, call 999 or 112 and ask for Police, then Mountain Rescue."]}]}, {"module": 5, "title": "Navigation, timing and weather limits", "lessons": [{"title": "Grid references: saying exactly where you are", "chapters": ["In case of emergency"], "points": []}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Safety, illness and calling for help", "lessons": [{"title": "Plan like a safe camper: gear, weather and knowledge", "chapters": ["In case of emergency"], "points": ["Agree an action time: if no call by 19:00, your friend rings you, and if no answer by 20:00 they call 999 and ask for Police, then Mountain Rescue."]}, {"title": "Calling for help", "chapters": ["In case of emergency"], "points": ["In the hills, dial 999 or 112 and ask for Police, then Mountain Rescue.", "Give all your prepared details and stay where you are until contacted.", "If you must call again, repeat the procedure in full.", "Location, with a grid reference if possible.", "Name, gender and age of the casualty."]}, {"title": "Minor injuries and heat: blisters, burns, sprains and stings", "chapters": ["In case of emergency"], "points": ["In the UK that means dialling 999 or 112."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "Rubric, run and review", "chapters": ["In case of emergency"], "points": []}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "met-mtn", "status": "unknown", "name": "Mountain safety", "by": "Met Office", "modules": [3, 5, 6, "cap"], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 3, "title": "The sleep system and staying warm", "lessons": [{"title": "How you lose heat: wind, height and wet", "chapters": ["Mountain safety"], "points": ["Air usually gets colder as you climb. The Met Office gives an average of about 2 °C of cooling per 300 metres, though it varies a lot.", "Wind also strengthens with height and speeds up over ridges.", "Average cooling of about 2 °C per 300 m, varies.", "450 ÷ 300 = 1.5 steps of 300 m. 1.5 × 2 °C = 3 °C of average cooling.", "The lapse rate is an average, not a promise. Cloud, wind and local ground can make the real number very different."]}]}, {"module": 5, "title": "Navigation, timing and weather limits", "lessons": [{"title": "How long will it take? Naismith's rule and the weather limits", "chapters": ["Mountain safety"], "points": ["Gusts over 30 or 40 mph can affect balance; over 50 mph walking is difficult; over 70 mph forward progress is almost impossible.", "Low cloud often means visibility under 50 m, so it is easy to lose direction. Use a map and compass; GPS should not be relied on entirely.", "On average about 2 °C cooler per 300 m of climb.", "Streams can rise through the day; an easy crossing in the morning may be impassable on the return."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Safety, illness and calling for help", "lessons": [{"title": "Plan like a safe camper: gear, weather and knowledge", "chapters": ["Mountain safety"], "points": ["The Met Office asks every hill-goer three questions before setting off. Do I have the right gear? Do I know what the weather will be like? Am I confident I have the knowledge and skills?", "Clothing including hat, gloves and extra layers, suitable footwear, map and compass, first aid kit, enough food and drink, a head torch and a whistle.", "Use the forecast to pick a day and route that suit your party. The Met Office issues a mountain forecast twice a day for ten upland areas.", "Know how to navigate, pitch and use the stove; take a course if not.", "Know when it will get dark and take a torch."]}, {"title": "Hazards on site and on the walk", "chapters": ["Mountain safety"], "points": ["The Met Office warns streams can rise through the day."]}, {"title": "Recognising illness: cold, gas and dirty water", "chapters": ["Mountain safety"], "points": ["The Met Office names Mountain Training's Hill Skills and Mountain Skills courses as a start for hill safety."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "The brief and the kit", "chapters": ["Mountain safety"], "points": ["Map, compass, whistle, first aid kit, torch."]}, {"title": "Rubric, run and review", "chapters": ["Mountain safety"], "points": ["A trip card, hazard list and call card."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "rei-pad", "status": "unknown", "name": "Sleeping Pads: How to Choose", "by": "REI Co-op Expert Advice (read in part)", "modules": [3, "cap"], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 3, "title": "The sleep system and staying warm", "lessons": [{"title": "Sleeping bags and what the numbers mean", "chapters": ["Sleeping Pads: How to Choose"], "points": ["Decision: if you sleep cold, choose a bag with a comfort rating of 4 °C or lower, or add a liner and a better pad. The rating also assumes a pad with R-value 4, covered in the next lesson."]}, {"title": "The pad under you: R-value and building a sleep system", "chapters": ["Sleeping Pads: How to Choose"], "points": ["The ground pulls heat out of you. A sleeping pad insulates you from it, and a good pad matters as much as the bag.", "R-value measures a pad's resistance to heat flow. A higher number means a warmer pad. A pad of R 2.0 is twice as warm as R 1.0.", "R-values add up if you stack pads.", "Closed-cell foam pads are mostly around R 2.", "Your sleep system has three parts: the bag, the pad and what you wear. Bag temperature ratings assume use with a pad of R 4."]}, {"title": "How you lose heat: wind, height and wet", "chapters": ["Sleeping Pads: How to Choose"], "points": []}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "The brief and the kit", "chapters": ["Sleeping Pads: How to Choose"], "points": ["Bag ratings assume a pad with R 4, so the two are a system."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "rei-bag", "status": "unknown", "name": "Understanding Sleeping Bag Temperature Ratings", "by": "Ken Knapp, REI Co-op Expert Advice", "modules": [3, "cap"], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 3, "title": "The sleep system and staying warm", "lessons": [{"title": "Sleeping bags and what the numbers mean", "chapters": ["Understanding Sleeping Bag Temperature Ratings"], "points": ["REI calls a sleeping bag's temperature rating the key factor in choosing one.", "But ratings are estimates, not guarantees.", "Real-world comfort probably will not match the lab figure because a lab cannot simulate every variable.", "Brands once tested bags their own way, so one firm's 30-degree bag was not the same as another's. The industry agreed a common method, and independent labs now assign ratings.", "EN (European Norm) came first, and ISO now oversees the near-identical test."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "The brief and the kit", "chapters": ["Understanding Sleeping Bag Temperature Ratings"], "points": ["Bag with a suitable rating, pad with a suitable R-value, warm hat, dry sleep clothes.", "Forecast low 6 °C at 200 m; bag comfort 3 °C; pad R 4.5."]}, {"title": "Rubric, run and review", "chapters": ["Understanding Sleeping Bag Temperature Ratings"], "points": []}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "met-wc", "status": "unknown", "name": "Wind chill factor", "by": "Met Office", "modules": [3], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 3, "title": "The sleep system and staying warm", "lessons": [{"title": "How you lose heat: wind, height and wet", "chapters": ["Wind chill factor"], "points": ["Air temperature on a forecast is only part of the story. The Met Office 'feels like' temperature adds wind speed and humidity at a person's height and an understanding of how the body loses heat.", "The UK method measures how much heat a bare face loses at a walking speed of 3 mph.", "On calm days the body warms a boundary layer of air next to the skin. Wind strips that layer away, so skin cools and you feel colder.", "The Met Office notes that feels-like temperatures through the year can be 3 or 4 degrees colder than the actual maximum.", "Strips the warm air layer next to skin."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "cdc", "status": "unknown", "name": "About Water Treatment Options When Hiking, Camping, or Traveling", "by": "US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, 30/01/2025", "modules": [4, 6, "cap"], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 4, "title": "The camp kitchen: stoves, water, food", "lessons": [{"title": "Making water safe", "chapters": ["About Water Treatment Options When Hiking, Camping, or Traveling"], "points": ["Clear water can still make you ill. The US Centers for Disease Control and Prevention (CDC) says germs from human or animal waste can get into lakes, rivers and streams, and that water from a clear mountain stream is generally not safe to drink untreated.", "If you are not sure, treat it.", "Boil: rolling boil for 1 minute (3 minutes above 6,500 ft).", "All germs: bacteria, viruses and parasites.", "Filter (1 micron or smaller, or NSF 53 or 58)."]}]}, {"module": 6, "title": "Safety, illness and calling for help", "lessons": [{"title": "Recognising illness: cold, gas and dirty water", "chapters": ["About Water Treatment Options When Hiking, Camping, or Traveling"], "points": ["Diarrhoea, vomiting, stomach pain."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "The brief and the kit", "chapters": ["About Water Treatment Options When Hiking, Camping, or Traveling"], "points": ["Water: tap, treated by site; boil as backup."]}, {"title": "Rubric, run and review", "chapters": ["About Water Treatment Options When Hiking, Camping, or Traveling"], "points": []}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "fsa", "status": "unknown", "name": "Camping food safety", "by": "Stuart Forward, Food Standards Agency blog, 20/07/2020", "modules": [4, "cap"], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 4, "title": "The camp kitchen: stoves, water, food", "lessons": [{"title": "Food, hygiene and keeping it cold", "chapters": ["Camping food safety"], "points": ["Camp food needs planning because your fridge is a cool box. The UK Food Standards Agency (FSA) says to take extra care with ready-to-eat foods such as cooked meat, eggs and dairy. Keep ready-to-eat and raw foods apart in sealed containers.", "Food should be stored below 5 °C.", "Store below 5 °C. A cool box may not hold that for long; use ice packs and check.", "Raw and ready-to-eat in sealed containers.", "Steaming hot throughout, no pink meat when cut into the thickest part, clear juices."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "The brief and the kit", "chapters": ["Camping food safety"], "points": ["Cool box with ice packs, separate containers for raw and ready-to-eat.", "Food: first-night sausages cooked until steaming, below 5 °C until cooked."]}, {"title": "Rubric, run and review", "chapters": ["Camping food safety"], "points": []}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "lgfl-cooker", "status": "unknown", "name": "The Nansen Cooker", "by": "London Grid for Learning, Polar Exploration in the Heroic Age of Scientific Discovery", "modules": [4], "note": "Polar Life KS1/2", "uses": [{"module": 4, "title": "The camp kitchen: stoves, water, food", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Fridtjof Nansen", "chapters": ["The Nansen Cooker (Polar Life KS1/2)"], "points": ["A schools resource adds that the cooker was compact and made of lightweight aluminium so it could be carried on a sledge, and that it warmed the tent as it heated the meal."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "os-comp", "status": "unknown", "name": "Beginners guide to using a compass", "by": "Jonathan Elder, OS GetOutside, 05/06/2022", "modules": [5], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 5, "title": "Navigation, timing and weather limits", "lessons": [{"title": "The compass: taking and following a bearing", "chapters": ["Beginners guide to using a compass"], "points": ["Even with a phone, a compass matters if technology fails or visibility is poor.", "The OS guide assumes a baseplate (orienteering) compass.", "Line up the edge of the compass between where you are (A) and where you want to go (B), with the direction-of-travel arrow pointing toward B.", "Turn the bezel until the orienting arrow lies parallel to the grid lines, pointing to grid north (the top of the map).", "Allow for magnetic variation, printed in the map key: turn the bezel anticlockwise to add a positive variation."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "brit-shack", "status": "unknown", "name": "Ernest Shackleton", "by": "Encyclopaedia Britannica", "modules": [5, "cap"], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 5, "title": "Navigation, timing and weather limits", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Ernest Shackleton", "chapters": ["Ernest Shackleton"], "points": ["Judge the result by the return. Britannica records that he turned back because supplies were running out, and that later, after the Endurance was lost, not one of the crew died.", "A successful turn-back is a success, not a failure."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Ernest Shackleton", "chapters": ["Ernest Shackleton"], "points": ["When the plan fails, protect the people. The Endurance was trapped in the ice and crushed, the crew camped on the ice and reached Elephant Island by boat, and Shackleton and five others sailed to South Georgia to bring help. Not one of the crew died.", "When a plan fails, protect people first, then learn."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "os-map", "status": "unknown", "name": "Map reading made easy", "by": "Ordnance Survey (booklet)", "modules": [5, "cap"], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 5, "title": "Navigation, timing and weather limits", "lessons": [{"title": "Reading a map: scale, symbols and distance", "chapters": ["Map reading made easy"], "points": ["A map is a drawing of an area, so you know what to expect and can tell whether you are going the right way.", "Symbols and colours stand for roads, buildings and rivers, and the key explains each one.", "OS maps are printed with north at the top.", "To remember the compass points, Ordnance Survey suggests a rhyme such as Naughty Elephants Squirt Water.", "Between the main points are north-east, south-east, south-west and north-west."]}, {"title": "Grid references: saying exactly where you are", "chapters": ["Map reading made easy"], "points": ["The blue lines on an OS map form the National Grid, which pinpoints a place anywhere on the map.", "Vertical lines are eastings and horizontal lines are northings.", "Great Britain is divided into 100 km squares, each named with two letters.", "Remember: along the corridor, then up the stairs. Read the eastings (across) first, then the northings (up).", "Find the easting line on the left of the square, e.g. 62."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "Rubric, run and review", "chapters": ["Map reading made easy"], "points": ["A six-figure reference for the pitch and a Naismith estimate for any walk.", "The navigation row asks for a six-figure reference, and the call card uses it."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "wiki-nimrod", "status": "unknown", "name": "Nimrod Expedition", "by": "Wikipedia", "modules": [5, "cap"], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 5, "title": "Navigation, timing and weather limits", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Ernest Shackleton", "chapters": ["Nimrod Expedition"], "points": ["Depot supplies for the return and count what you carry. On the Nimrod march, by late December the party carried about a month of food, having stored the rest in depots for the return.", "Do the sum honestly. They could not cover the remaining distance and back on that amount.", "Decide on the plan, not the feeling. On 4 January 1909 Shackleton accepted that the Pole was beyond them and revised the goal.", "Turn back with margin. The march ended on 9 January, 97 geographical miles from the Pole. In South he writes that they were beaten by stress of circumstances within ninety-seven miles of their goal.", "Wikipedia's account of the Nimrod march is a secondary source. The preface to South is Shackleton's own statement of the 97-mile figure. We did not read the diary accounts of the march."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Ernest Shackleton", "chapters": ["Nimrod Expedition"], "points": ["Present the plan and get it challenged. Shackleton presented his plans for the Nimrod expedition to the Royal Geographical Society in February 1907.", "Budget the time to prepare. He then had about six months to raise money, equip a ship and recruit the party.", "Fix the goal and revise it when the sums say so. On the Nimrod march he accepted on 4 January that the Pole was beyond them.", "The Shackleton column follows the sources. The right column is our adaptation.", "He planned with depots, advice and clear leadership."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "shack-south", "status": "part", "name": "South", "by": "Ernest Shackleton, ibiblio", "modules": [5, "cap"], "note": "1919), full text (preface and chapter I read in this pass", "uses": [{"module": 5, "title": "Navigation, timing and weather limits", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Ernest Shackleton", "chapters": ["South (1919), full text (preface and chapter I read in this pass)"], "points": ["We read the preface and the opening of his own book South (1919), and Wikipedia and Britannica on the Nimrod expedition. The turn-back facts come from Wikipedia and his preface, so the record is partial.", "Ask those who know the ground before you decide the route. In South he describes consulting the whaling captains at South Georgia about the ice, and he says their advice was worth attention and shaped his decision to steer for the South Sandwich group before pushing south.", "He took advice from the whaling captains.", "Ask those who know the ground first."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Ernest Shackleton", "chapters": ["South (1919), full text (preface and chapter I read in this pass)"], "points": ["We read his preface and opening chapter in South and secondary pages from Britannica and Wikipedia. The record is partial.", "Take local knowledge. For the Endurance voyage he consulted the whaling captains about the ice and weighed their advice in choosing the route.", "Take the leadership seriously. In South he writes that a leader is helped greatly if there is no uncertainty in the minds of those who follow.", "He took advice from the whaling captains."]}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "os-naismith", "status": "unknown", "name": "Why we're adjusting Naismith's Rule", "by": "Ordnance Survey blog, 09/05/2019", "modules": [5, "cap"], "note": "", "uses": [{"module": 5, "title": "Navigation, timing and weather limits", "lessons": [{"title": "How long will it take? Naismith's rule and the weather limits", "chapters": ["Why we're adjusting Naismith's Rule"], "points": ["Naismith's rule is a simple way to estimate walking time. In metric terms: allow one hour for every 5 km and add one hour for every 600 m of ascent. In imperial: one hour per 3 miles plus one hour per 2,000 ft of ascent.", "It survives because it is simple and needs only a map and a piece of string.", "Distance: 10 ÷ 5 = 2 hours.", "Ascent: 450 ÷ 600 = 0.75 hours.", "OS says corrections have been proposed over the years, for example for fitness or slope, and is working on a more personalised model. The rule is a rule of thumb, not a guarantee. Being overdue or stuck out after dark still accounts for a sizeable number of mountain rescue incidents each year."]}]}, {"module": "cap", "title": "Plan, pack and review a first overnight camp", "lessons": [{"title": "Rubric, run and review", "chapters": ["Why we're adjusting Naismith's Rule"], "points": []}]}], "origin": ""}, {"key": "nightingale", "status": "unknown", "name": "Notes on Nursing: What It Is, and What It Is Not", "by": "Florence Nightingale, Project Gutenberg #17366", "modules": [6], "note": "1859, full text", "uses": [{"module": 6, "title": "Safety, illness and calling for help", "lessons": [{"title": "The lecturer's method: Florence Nightingale", "chapters": ["Notes on Nursing: What It Is, and What It Is Not (1859), full text"], "points": ["Florence Nightingale published Notes on Nursing in 1859. We read parts of its full text, including its opening on air and on what causes disease, its chapter on variety, and its passages on observation. The record is rich for these topics.", "She wrote for nurses in homes and hospitals, not for hikers. We adapt her method to first aid in the outdoors, and the adaptation is ours.", "Name what is missing before you name the disease. She wrote that when a patient is cold, feverish or faint, the cause is often a want of fresh air, light, warmth, quiet, cleanliness, or punctuality and care in diet, and that disease is a reparative process hindered by such wants.", "Make air the first check. The very first canon of nursing, she says, is to keep the air the patient breathes as pure as the external air without chilling him.", "Observe for a purpose. The most important practical lesson, she says, is to teach nurses what to observe, how to observe, what shows improvement and what the reverse. Observation is not for piling up facts but for saving life and increasing health and comfort, and she says what is wanted is facts, not opinions."]}]}], "origin": ""}], "sections": [{"title": "Book sources (19/11/2026)", "items": ["First review: Scottish Outdoor Access Code 2005, Countryside Code (GOV.UK), OS Map reading made easy and the CDC Yellow Book water chapter, each read in full.", "Second review, 19/11/2026:", "Darwall v Dartmoor National Park Authority [2025] UKSC 20: read in full. Module 1 now teaches the legal basis of Dartmoor wild camping (section 10(1), Dartmoor Commons Act 1985, subject to the byelaws).", "UKHSA Be Tick Aware toolkit (2023): read in full. Module 6 now teaches ticks and Lyme disease; Module 2 adds ticks to pitch choice. The toolkit gives two different tick species counts, so the course states none.", "Aerie Wilderness Medicine, 15th edition (US): read in part, 19 of 68 chapters. Clinical first-responder skills, US law, altitude illness, medications and survival chapters were skipped with reasons. It feeds hypothermia staging and the wrap (Module 3), trench foot (Module 3), stove burns and carbon monoxide (Module 4), thunderstorms (Module 5), and a new Module 6 lesson on blisters, burns, sprains, stings and heat. UK emergency numbers come from Mountain Rescue England and Wales, not the US manual. Aerie's boil times are internally inconsistent, so Module 4 keeps the CDC figures.", "Findings across all reviews: 68 logged; 56 applied, 7 confirmed, 3 avoided, 2 skipped.", "Still web-only: tents, sleeping bags and pads."]}], "counts": {"full": 7, "part": 4, "search": 0, "none": 0, "unknown": 33}, "hasMd": true};
